    role: 'Powerful rivals of the gods — not simply demons',
    summary: '"Asura" begins in the Rigveda as an honorific for great gods and only later comes to mean the rivals of the devas. Purāṇic asuras include tyrants and devotees alike; in Buddhism the asura realm is a rebirth marked by jealousy; in Jainism asurakumāras are a class of gods.',
    overview: 'The asuras are one of South Asia\'s great mythic collectives. In Hindu narrative they battle the devas for cosmic power — most famously during the churning of the ocean for the nectar of immortality — and individual asuras (Hiraṇyakaśipu, Mahiṣa) are slain by Viṣṇu or the Goddess. Yet the asura Prahlāda is a model devotee of Viṣṇu and the asura king Bali is praised for his generosity.',
    origins: 'The Rigveda applies asura as a title to gods such as Varuṇa; the word is cognate with Avestan ahura, as in Ahura Mazdā. In the Brāhmaṇas, devas and asuras are rival offspring of Prajāpati, and the asuras lose through arrogance — offering sacrifice into their own mouths (Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa 5.1.1.1).',
    religious: 'Buddhism counts the asura realm among the destinies of rebirth: its beings are powerful but consumed by envy of the gods and endless conflict (in Japan, the Ashura of Kōfuku-ji is a protector of the Dharma). Jain cosmology lists asurakumāras among the bhavanavāsī ("dwelling") gods — beings within the karmic cosmos rather than enemies of a creator.',
    symbolism: 'The tug-of-war over the ocean of milk, the drawn sword. Asuras symbolize power turned against harmony — but also the possibility that the "other side" can hold devotion and virtue.',
