    summary: 'Chinese and Japanese traditions distinguish ghosts of the dead, gods, uncanny transforming creatures and Buddhist "māras." English "demon" blurs these: an oni, a gui and a yōkai are different kinds of thing.',
    overview: 'Chinese religion separates guǐ (鬼, ghosts of the dead), shén (神, gods and spirits), and yāo or yāoguài (妖怪, uncanny creatures that gain powers, such as foxes). Buddhism added mó (魔), a word coined from the transliteration of Māra. Japan read the same characters differently: 鬼 became oni, 妖怪 became yōkai, and kami — the gods of Shintō — can be gentle or wrathful.',
    origins: 'Ancestor ritual has shaped Chinese ideas of ghosts since the Shang and Zhou dynasties: properly honoured dead become ancestors, neglected or violently killed dead become dangerous ghosts. Daoist ritual masters and the demon-queller Zhong Kui developed exorcistic traditions. In Japan, Heian-period fears of vengeful spirits (onryō) led to the deification of the courtier Sugawara no Michizane as the god Tenjin.',
    religious: 'Responses are largely ritual and social: feeding ghosts, appeasing or enshrining angry spirits, expelling misfortune at festivals such as Setsubun. Korea offers a further contrast: dokkaebi are mischievous goblins who can reward as well as trick, while gwisin are ghosts of the dead.',
    symbolism: 'The ancestral altar, the paper talisman, the festival of expulsion. East Asian demonology symbolizes relationship — the ongoing obligations between the living, the dead and the land.',
    attributes: { "Ghosts": 'Gui (China), yūrei (Japan), gwisin (Korea)', "Uncanny beings": 'Yaoguai, yōkai, dokkaebi', "Buddhist": 'Mó / ma, from Māra', "Typical response": 'Feeding, appeasing, expelling' },
    interpretations: 'Folklorists such as Michael Dylan Foster caution that yōkai is a modern umbrella term; Teiser and others show how Buddhist and native ideas fused in the ghost festival.',
