    summary: 'Yōkai is an umbrella term for Japan\'s strange creatures, spirits and transformations — from the river-dwelling kappa to haunted household objects. Some are dangerous, many are mischievous, some bring luck: yōkai are not a class of demons.',
    overview: 'Yōkai range from frightening (the kappa that drowns swimmers) to harmless or even benevolent (the zashiki-warashi, a child-spirit said to bring a household prosperity). Medieval picture-scrolls show the hyakki yagyō, the "night parade of one hundred demons," including tsukumogami — old tools that come to life.',
    origins: 'Toriyama Sekien\'s illustrated catalogue "Gazu Hyakki Yagyō" (1776) and its sequels gave many yōkai a fixed visual form. In the Meiji period Inoue Enryō\'s "yōkaigaku" (yōkai studies) popularized the word itself while trying to explain such beliefs away; folklorist Yanagita Kunio collected them; manga artist Mizuki Shigeru made them a modern cultural treasure.',
    religious: 'Yōkai overlap with, but are not identical to, kami (gods), yūrei (ghosts) and Buddhist demons. Their moral status is local and situational — the same creature may be feared in one village and honoured in another.',
    symbolism: 'The night parade, the transformed object, the creature at the edge of the village. Yōkai symbolize the uncanny side of everyday life and nature.',
    attributes: { "Examples": 'Kappa, tengu, kitsune, tsukumogami, zashiki-warashi', "Catalogue": 'Toriyama Sekien (1776)', "Coined/popularized": 'Inoue Enryō (Meiji)', "Moral status": 'Varies widely' },
    associations: [
      { theme: 'Fear', layer: 'folklore', basis: 'Many yōkai tales warn of dangerous creatures of rivers, mountains and night.' },
      { theme: 'Trickery', layer: 'folklore', basis: 'Shape-changers such as tanuki and kitsune are famed for pranks and illusions.' },
      { theme: 'Moral ambivalence', layer: 'scholarly', basis: 'Folklorists (Foster 2015; Komatsu 2017) stress that yōkai range from harmful to beneficent.' },
    ],
    interpretations: 'Foster reads yōkai as a lens on how Japanese culture has thought about the unknown, from medieval scrolls to Pokémon.',
