    role: 'The horned ogre-demon of Japan — punisher, raider, and guardian',
    summary: 'Oni are Japan\'s horned, club-wielding ogres: wardens of the Buddhist hells, raiders in legend, and figures driven out each year at Setsubun. Yet oni-faced roof tiles guard buildings, and some oni-like visitors are honoured as deities.',
    overview: 'The classic oni is a large horned figure with red or blue skin, a tiger-skin loincloth and an iron club (kanabō). In Buddhist imagery oni torment sinners in hell, most famously the ox-headed and horse-headed wardens. Heian and medieval legend tells of the oni Shuten-dōji of Mount Ōe, killed by the warrior Minamoto no Raikō.',
    origins: 'The 10th-century dictionary Wamyō Ruijushō suggests the word oni comes from on (隠), "hidden." The Izumo Fudoki (733 CE) tells of a one-eyed oni that devoured a farmer. The horns and tiger skin are often linked to the northeast "demon gate" (kimon), the ushitora (ox-tiger) direction.',
    religious: 'At Setsubun, people throw roasted beans shouting "Oni wa soto! Fuku wa uchi!" ("Demons out! Luck in!"). Onigawara roof tiles use the oni face protectively, and the Namahage of Oga Peninsula — masked visitors who scold idle children — are regarded as deities. The Noh hannya mask shows a woman transformed into an oni by jealousy.',
    symbolism: 'Horns, the iron club, the bean-throwing. Oni symbolize disorder and misfortune that must be expelled — and power that, turned outward, can protect.',
