    overview: 'The earliest Japanese fox-wife tale, in the Nihon Ryōiki (c. 822), explains the word kitsune as "come and sleep." White foxes guard Inari shrines across Japan as messengers of the deity. Other legends feature malevolent foxes: Tamamo-no-Mae, a nine-tailed fox disguised as a court lady, whose spirit was said to dwell in the "killing stone" (Sesshō-seki).',
