    classification: 'Concept',
    color: EAST,
    role: 'Layered worlds of local spirits, ghosts and world religions',
    summary: 'Southeast Asian spirit beliefs layer local animism with Hindu-Buddhist, Islamic and Christian frameworks. Spirits (Thai phi, Malay hantu, Philippine anito/diwata) can be ancestral, territorial, protective or dangerous — "demon" rarely fits.',
    overview: 'In Thailand and Laos, phi include guardian spirits housed in the spirit houses (san phra phum) outside homes, as well as dangerous ghosts such as the krasue and phi pop. In the Malay world, hantu include the pontianak, the penanggalan and the toyol, while healers (bomoh, dukun) mediate with them. In the Philippines, the aswang "complex" of shape-shifting predators coexists with nature spirits such as diwata.',
    origins: 'Many of these beliefs predate the arrival of Hinduism, Buddhism, Islam and Christianity, and were then reinterpreted by them. Spanish missionaries in the Philippines, for example, described local spirit specialists as agents of the devil — a colonial reframing scholars now study critically.',
    religious: 'Several dangerous beings in the region are specifically linked to women, pregnancy and childbirth — the pontianak, krasue and manananggal — reflecting the real dangers of birth and the social policing of women.',
    symbolism: 'The spirit house, the frangipani scent, the detaching head. Southeast Asian spirit lore symbolizes the presence of the dead and the land in everyday life.',
    attributes: { "Thai/Lao": 'Phi (spirits), spirit houses', "Malay/Indonesian": 'Hantu; bomoh / dukun', "Philippine": 'Aswang complex; anito, diwata', "Layering": 'Animism + world religions' },
    interpretations: 'Film scholars note the vitality of these figures in regional horror cinema; feminist scholars examine their gendered meanings.',
