    color: EAST,
    role: 'The vengeful ghost of a woman who died in pregnancy or childbirth',
    summary: 'The pontianak (Malaysia, Singapore, Brunei) and kuntilanak (Indonesia) are closely related female ghosts — usually of a woman who died in pregnancy or childbirth — known by long hair, a white dress, the scent of frangipani and a baby\'s cry. The names are not interchangeable everywhere, and older Malay lore distinguished several such spirits.',
    overview: 'In popular telling the pontianak appears as a beautiful woman who, when approached, reveals a monstrous form and attacks men or threatens pregnant women and infants. Malay lore holds that driving a nail into the hole at the nape of her neck turns her into a normal woman. In Indonesia the kuntilanak is a similar ghost, often said to haunt trees and to laugh; Indonesian folklore also has the distinct sundel bolong, with a hole in her back.',
    origins: 'Walter Skeat\'s "Malay Magic" (1900) recorded a more complex scheme: the langsuir was a woman who died in childbirth, and the pontianak (from mati-anak, "dead child") was her stillborn child — distinctions that later popular usage merged. The Indonesian city of Pontianak (founded 1771) has a founding legend in which Sultan Syarif Abdurrahman drove off kuntilanak with cannon fire.',
    religious: 'These are ghosts of the human dead, not fallen angels. Responses are folk-religious: Islamic prayer and recitation, local healers, protective objects. Since the Malay film "Pontianak" (1957) and Indonesian kuntilanak films, the figure has been a pillar of regional horror cinema.',
    symbolism: 'Frangipani, the white dress, the crying infant, the nail. The pontianak symbolizes maternal death and grief turned to vengeance — and anxieties about women\'s bodies and power.',
    attributes: { "Malaysia/Singapore": 'Pontianak', "Indonesia": 'Kuntilanak', "Older Malay lore": 'Langsuir (mother) / pontianak (stillborn child)', "Signs": 'Frangipani scent, baby\'s cry' },
