    summary: 'The krasue is a woman by day who at night detaches her head — with her heart and entrails hanging beneath — and flies in search of blood, raw flesh or filth, threatening pregnant women and newborns. Related figures appear across mainland and maritime Southeast Asia under different names.',
    overview: 'Thai tradition explains the krasue as the result of a curse, of bad karma, or of an inherited spirit (phi) passed from woman to woman. She glows as she flies; households placed thorny branches around the house so her entrails would snag. The Malay penanggalan is a closely related figure, said to soak her entrails in vinegar to shrink them before returning to her body.',
    origins: 'The krasue is part of the oral folklore of the Thai, Lao and Khmer worlds; Skeat (1900) recorded the penanggalan in Malaya. Thai cinema has repeatedly revived the figure, from mid-20th-century films to "Krasue Valentine" (2006) and "Inhuman Kiss" (2019).',
